# The golden corpus is BYTE-EXACT TEST DATA, not ordinary text.
#
#   *.world.yaml     tests/test_world_file.cpp compares each one against a
#                    fresh spade::world_to_yaml() emission, which always writes
#                    LF.
#   *.scene.yaml     tests/test_scene_file.cpp compares each one against a
#                    fresh spade::scene::scene_to_yaml() emission, which also
#                    always writes LF.
#   *.scenario.yaml  tests/test_determinism.cpp loads each one and runs it
#                    against the digest the file itself carries. Line endings
#                    do not reach a digest, but these files are authored,
#                    reviewed and diffed alongside the world files they name,
#                    and one corpus with two line-ending rules is a trap.
#
# This repository's root .gitattributes sets `* text=auto`, and this box has
# core.autocrlf=true, so without the lines below a checkout would rewrite these
# files with CRLF endings and the world-file comparisons would fail on Windows
# while passing on Linux CI. eol=lf (rather than -text) keeps the files
# diffable and reviewable -- they exist to be read.
#
# ONE FILE FOR THE WHOLE CORPUS, at its root: S5 Task 7 consolidated the
# worlds-local .gitattributes here when the scenarios subdirectory joined, so
# that a future golden/<something>/ inherits the rule instead of needing to
# remember it. `git check-attr text eol -- <path>` is how to confirm coverage.
*.world.yaml text eol=lf
*.scenario.yaml text eol=lf
*.scene.yaml text eol=lf
